[{"text":"ex5 = DiracTrace[GA[\\[Rho], \\[Sigma]] . GA[5] . GALR[{\\[Mu], \\[Nu]}]]","anchor":"cb28","section":"Dirac matrices with light-cone components"},{"text":"\\[\\text{tr}\\left(\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\rho }.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\sigma }.\\bar{\\gamma }^5.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\mu }{}_{\\perp }.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\nu }{}_{\\perp }\\right)\\]","anchor":"cb28-1","section":"Dirac matrices with light-cone components"},{"text":"ex5 // DiracSimplify","anchor":"cb29","section":"Dirac matrices with light-cone components"},{"text":"\\[2 i \\overline{n}^{\\rho } \\bar{\\epsilon }^{\\mu _{\\perp }\\nu _{\\perp }\\sigma _{\\perp }\\;\\overline{\\text{nb}}}+2 i \\overline{\\text{nb}}^{\\rho } \\bar{\\epsilon }^{\\mu _{\\perp }\\nu _{\\perp }\\sigma _{\\perp }\\overline{n}}-2 i \\overline{n}^{\\sigma } \\bar{\\epsilon }^{\\mu _{\\perp }\\nu _{\\perp }\\rho _{\\perp }\\;\\overline{\\text{nb}}}-i \\overline{n}^{\\sigma } \\overline{\\text{nb}}^{\\rho } \\bar{\\epsilon }^{\\mu _{\\perp }\\nu _{\\perp }\\overline{n}\\;\\overline{\\text{nb}}}-2 i \\overline{\\text{nb}}^{\\sigma } \\bar{\\epsilon }^{\\mu _{\\perp }\\nu _{\\perp }\\rho _{\\perp }\\overline{n}}+i \\overline{n}^{\\rho } \\overline{\\text{nb}}^{\\sigma } \\bar{\\epsilon }^{\\mu _{\\perp }\\nu _{\\perp }\\overline{n}\\;\\overline{\\text{nb}}}-4 i \\bar{\\epsilon }^{\\mu _{\\perp }\\nu _{\\perp }\\rho _{\\perp }\\sigma _{\\perp }}\\]","anchor":"cb29-1","section":"Dirac matrices with light-cone components"},{"text":"In order to handle Dirac matrices involving light-cone components effectively, one needs to define some ordering. The current (hard-coded) choice is that the perpendicular component always get anticommuted to the very right of each Dirac chain, while every remaining occurence of \\((\\gamma \\cdot \\bar{n}) (\\gamma \\cdot n)\\) is changed to \\((\\gamma \\cdot n) (\\gamma \\cdot \\bar{n})\\).","anchor":"cb29-1","section":"Dirac matrices with light-cone components"},{"text":"In some calculations one might end up with a mixture of explicit light-cone components and generic Lorentz tensors. If those tensors admit a particularly simple representation in terms of light-cone components, it can be enforced using the function ToLightConeComponents","anchor":"introducing-light-cone-components-by-hand","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"For example, the following expression cannot be simplified any further","anchor":"introducing-light-cone-components-by-hand","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"ex6 = GS[nb, vp]","anchor":"cb30","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"\\[\\left(\\bar{\\gamma }\\cdot \\overline{\\text{nb}}\\right).\\left(\\bar{\\gamma }\\cdot \\overline{\\text{vp}}\\right)\\]","anchor":"cb30-1","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"Now let us suppose that (v')^{\\mu} can be actually written as \\(\\alpha n^\\mu + \\bar{n}^{\\mu}/(4 \\alpha)\\). We can implement this as follows","anchor":"cb30-1","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"SP[vp, nb] = 2*alpha; SP[vp, n] = 2*1/(4 alpha); LightConePerpendicularComponent[Momentum[vp], Momentum[n], Momentum[nb]] = 0;","anchor":"cb31","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"FV[vp, mu] % // ToLightConeComponents","anchor":"cb32","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"\\overline{\\text{vp}}^{\\text{mu}}","anchor":"cb32-2","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"\\[\\text{alpha} \\overline{n}^{\\text{mu}}+\\frac{\\overline{\\text{nb}}^{\\text{mu}}}{4 \\;\\text{alpha}}\\]","anchor":"cb32-2","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"However, this will not make FeynCalc automatically simplify the Dirac chain","anchor":"cb32-2","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"ex6 // DiracSimplify","anchor":"cb33","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"\\[\\left(\\bar{\\gamma }\\cdot \\overline{\\text{nb}}\\right).\\left(\\bar{\\gamma }\\cdot \\overline{\\text{vp}}\\right)\\]","anchor":"cb33-1","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"Using ToLightConeComponents we can explicitly rewrite vp in the chain in terms of the light-cone components and hence enforce the desired simplification. In fact, the function will also automatically simplify some common expressions such \\gamma \\cdot \\bar{n} \\gamma \\cdot \\bar{n} = \\gamma \\cdot n \\gamma \\cdot n = 0","anchor":"cb33-1","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"ex6 // ToLightConeComponents % // DiracSimplify","anchor":"cb34","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"\\[\\text{alpha} \\left(\\bar{\\gamma }\\cdot \\overline{\\text{nb}}\\right).\\left(\\bar{\\gamma }\\cdot \\overline{n}\\right)\\]","anchor":"cb34-2","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"\\[4 \\;\\text{alpha}-\\text{alpha} \\left(\\bar{\\gamma }\\cdot \\overline{n}\\right).\\left(\\bar{\\gamma }\\cdot \\overline{\\text{nb}}\\right)\\]","anchor":"cb34-2","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"Such simplifications inside ToLightConeComponents can be disabled using the option DotSimplify","anchor":"cb34-2","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"ex6 // ToLightConeComponents[#, DotSimplify -> False] & % // DiracSimplify","anchor":"cb35","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"\\[\\left(\\bar{\\gamma }\\cdot \\overline{\\text{nb}}\\right).\\left(\\text{alpha} \\bar{\\gamma }\\cdot \\overline{n}+\\frac{\\bar{\\gamma }\\cdot \\overline{\\text{nb}}}{4 \\;\\text{alpha}}\\right)\\]","anchor":"cb35-2","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"\\[4 \\;\\text{alpha}-\\text{alpha} \\left(\\bar{\\gamma }\\cdot \\overline{n}\\right).\\left(\\bar{\\gamma }\\cdot \\overline{\\text{nb}}\\right)\\]","anchor":"cb35-2","section":"Introducing light-cone components by hand"},{"text":"int = FVLRD[p, \\[Mu]] SFAD[p, p - q]","anchor":"cb36","section":"Reductions of loop integrals with numerators involving light-cone components"},{"text":"\\[\\frac{p^{\\mu }{}_{\\perp }}{(p^2+i \\eta ).((p-q)^2+i \\eta )}\\]","anchor":"cb36-1","section":"Reductions of loop integrals with numerators involving light-cone components"},{"text":"TID[int, p]","anchor":"cb37","section":"Reductions of loop integrals with numerators involving light-cone components"},{"text":"\\[\\frac{q^{\\mu }{}_{\\perp }}{2 (p^2+i \\eta ).((p-q)^2+i \\eta )}\\]","anchor":"cb37-1","section":"Reductions of loop integrals with numerators involving light-cone components"},{"text":"FourDivergence cannot yet differentiate w.r.t light-cone components directly. However, the same effect can be easily achieved by first differentiating w.r.t the usual 4-momentum and then contracting the free index with the corresponding metric tensor","anchor":"differentiations","section":"Differentiations"},{"text":"ex = FV[p1, \\[Mu]]/SP[p1]","anchor":"cb38","section":"Differentiations"},{"text":"\\[\\frac{\\overline{\\text{p1}}^{\\mu }}{\\overline{\\text{p1}}^2}\\]","anchor":"cb38-1","section":"Differentiations"}]